Certified Minimal-Prime Branch Closures for Odd Perfect Numbers  Marco Mantovanelli Independent Researcher  marco@mantovanelli.de  Abstract  For an odd perfect number   N   , write   q   = min { p   :   p   |   N   }   for its smallest prime divisor. This paper closes the five certified minimal-prime branches   q   = 5 ,   7 ,   11 ,   13 ,   17 . This is a least-prime- divisor assertion: it does not rule out divisibility by one of these primes for a hypothetical odd perfect number whose smallest prime divisor is smaller. Nor does it address the branches   q   = 3   or  q   ≥   19 . In each of the five branches, every case generated from the exact   q -adic valuation balance for   σ ( N   ) = 2 N   and the lower-prime avoidance constraints terminates before a compatible branch realization can occur.   The valuation balance specifies the   q -adic contribution that must be supplied by cyclotomic factors   σ ( p e ) , while lower-prime avoidance forbids any support prime below   q   from appearing in those factors. Together these conditions reduce each branch to finite coverage splits in the first relevant input prime or Euler-residue class. At the terminal records, the certificates then verify the remaining arithmetic obstruction:   a residual cofactor forces additional support primes, a pure cyclotomic equation has only excluded solutions, an endpoint factorization/order check closes an exceptional case, or an exact tail-abundance product stays below   2 . The   q   = 5   branch is presented as the detailed audit model; the remaining four branches are then closed by the same forced-or-pure mechanism and by the included JSONL/Python certificates.  Keywords.   Odd perfect numbers; divisor sums; cyclotomic factors;   p -adic valuations; minimal prime divisor; branch-and-bound certification; computer-assisted proof.  MSC 2020.   Primary 11A25; Secondary 11Y05, 11Y16.  1   Introduction  An odd perfect number is a positive odd integer   N   satisfying  σ ( N   ) = 2 N,  where   σ   denotes the sum of divisors. The existence of odd perfect numbers is one of the classical open problems in number theory; Deza’s book [Dez22] gives a broad account of the surrounding perfect- number and amicable-number landscape, and Cai’s monograph [Cai22] surveys perfect numbers alongside Fibonacci sequences and related recurrences. Euler proved that any such   N   has the form  N   =   π α M   2 ,   π   ≡   α   ≡   1   (mod 4) ,   ( π, M   ) = 1 ,  so exactly one prime divisor of   N   , called the Euler prime, occurs to an odd exponent. 1  arXiv:2607.04365v1 [math.GM] 5 Jul 2026

The modern literature supplies strong global filters, but not a complete nonexistence proof. Writing   p 1   < p 2   <   · · ·   < p k   for the distinct prime divisors of a hypothetical odd perfect number, so that   p 1   is the smallest prime divisor and   k   =   ω ( N   ) , Grün proved the general least-prime bound  p 1   <   2 3   k   + 2  [Grü52].   Nielsen proved that every odd perfect number has at least nine distinct prime factors, and at least twelve if   3   ∤   N   , and later developed related Diophantine and upper-bound methods [Nie07, Nie15]. These results are important global constraints, but they do not by themselves reduce the least-prime-divisor problem to the cases   q   = 3 ,   5 ,   7 , since Grün’s upper bound increases with  ω ( N   ) .   Ochem and Rao proved the landmark lower bound   N   >   10 1500   and strong multiplicity constraints [OR12]. Goto–Ohno proved   p k   >   10 8 , while Iannucci proved   p k − 1   >   10 4   and   p k − 2   >   100  [GO08, Ian99, Ian00].   In the opposite direction, Zelinsky proved   p k − 1   <   (2 N   ) 1 / 5   and   p k − 1 p k   <  6 1 / 4 N   1 / 2 , and Bibby–Vyncke–Zelinsky proved   p k − 2   <   (2 N   ) 1 / 6   and   p k − 2 p k − 1 p k   <   (2 N   ) 3 / 5   [Zel19, BVZ21]. For prime-factor counts, Zelinsky and Clayton–Hansen sharpened inequalities relating the number   ω ( N   )   of distinct prime factors to the total number   Ω( N   )   counted with multiplicity; for example Clayton–Hansen proved  99 37   ω ( N   )   −   187 37   ≤   Ω( N   ) ,   3   ∤   N   ⇒   46 19   ω ( N   )   −   51 19   ≤   Ω( N   )  [Zel21, CH23].   Zelinsky also obtained lower bounds for the number of distinct prime factors in terms of the second, third, and fourth smallest prime factors, Stone proved improved product bounds and   p 1   <   3 k/ 7 + 3   for the smallest prime divisor, and Zelinsky bounded the reciprocal sum  T   ( N   ) =   P  p | N   1 /p   and the product   H ( N   ) =   Q  p | N   p/ ( p   −   1)   in terms of   p 1   [Zel23, Sto24, Zel25]. The result proved here is different in kind from these global filters. It is not a new lower bound for   N   , for   ω ( N   ) , or for the largest prime divisors, and it is not a divisibility exclusion such as “ 5   ∤   N   ”. It is an exact closure of five least-prime-divisor branches. Thus the   q   = 5   branch is the case   5   |   N  and   3   ∤   N   ; a hypothetical odd perfect number divisible by both   3   and   5   would belong to the   q   = 3  branch, not to the   q   = 5   branch. To the author’s knowledge, the sources cited above do not state the finite exhaustion of the exact minimal-prime branches   q   = 5 ,   7 ,   11 ,   13 ,   17   in the certificate sense used below. In this paper, published results serve only as branch filters and consistency checks; the closures themselves are driven by valuation-labeled cyclotomic input and finite certificates. We restrict attention to a finite list of minimal-prime branches.   Write   S   =   { p   :   p   |   N   } ,  q   = min   S , and   N   =   Q  p ∈ S   p e p   . Thus the branch   q   = 5 , for example, means that   5   |   N   and   3   ∤   N   , not merely that   5   |   N   . The main theorem proved here is the following certificate theorem.  Theorem 1.1   (Certified small minimal-prime closure) .   Relative to the explicitly listed verifier con- tract and the frozen certificate release   C small   described in Section 7, there is no odd perfect number with   min { p   :   p   |   N   } ∈ { 5 ,   7 ,   11 ,   13 ,   17 } . Equivalently, the five minimal-prime branch inventories in  C small   are exhausted.  The theorem is intentionally scoped. It is not an unconditional proof that odd perfect numbers do not exist. The cases   q   = 3   and   q   ≥   19   remain outside the scope of this paper. The branch   q   = 3  has no lower-prime avoidance layer and therefore behaves differently from all branches treated here. The residual branch   q   ≥   19   requires a separate treatment of the possible first   q -adic input orders and is left for future work. The contribution of this paper is threefold. First, it isolates the minimal-prime branch problem from the full odd-perfect-number problem.   Second, it gives a uniform valuation-and-avoidance 2

